<<~ kanawai uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119`>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! The W26 floor lane ~ one owner per registry, the floor by CID

Roadmap row `W26` carries the build of two operator rulings: //the system wikis release; every other wiki reads the floor// (2026-10-16) and //one owner per registry; the floor reads by CID// (2026-10-18), both at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road`>> #/rulings. Rows `W21` and `P8` ride it. The law it enacts already stands at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/wiki-layer-ontology`>> #/oracle-planes: an oracle names documents at one scope, that scope's owner writes its registry, and a registry travels no wider than its scope.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-shape`>>

!! The shape

```text
  CONTENT PLANE (CAS)   immutable bytes by CID: engine, grammar, plugins, corpus snapshots.
                        Anyone reads; nobody writes in place; a herm carries and never reads private bytes.

  FLOOR                 a manifest CID naming the lares and lararium snapshots and the engine.
                        Read-only and history-free. Genesis carries the first floor.
  RELEASE ANSWER        one per Nexus, on its crossroads board: the Nexus AID, the charter epoch,
                        the release before it, the floor manifest CID. The steward seals it; the
                        cabal approves its exact bytes; a herm carries it and signs nothing.
  oracle-floor               the floor's read-face, mounted under every wiki by the pin its face
                        accepted. Never a document any vessel writes.

  REGISTRIES, each written by its own scope's owner alone
    vessel bootstrap    this vessel: its daemon url, its own document pointers, its face planes,
                        its accepted floor pin per (face, Nexus) as sealed hot rows
    catalog-plane            the operator's documents, at the operator's scope
    Kahu realm          the cabal: the LIVE lares and lararium documents and their drafts
    crossroads board    the Nexus: its one public answer, the release

  A WIKI                a recipe over bags: oracle-floor floor (pinned CID, read-only) < libraries
                        (read-only) < its own canon (promotion only) < working-slot (every save
                        lands here) < draft-slot, personal-slot, temp-slot. An edit copies up; a promotion
                        moves down by receipt; system canon moves only as a Kahu-cabal promotion
                        in the realm, and reaches everyone else only as a release.
```

''What the shape answers.'' The `lares` and `lararium` documents live in one place, the Kahu realm of the Nexus whose cabal writes them; on a lone hearth that realm belongs to its operator, who stands as its own Kahu. Every other vessel holds their snapshots by CID. A vessel's own pointers rest in its own bootstrap, so no shared document hosts another vessel's url and nothing merges across siblings. A wiki's floor moves only on a person's accept of a signed release, face by face, so one vessel may hold two Nexuses' floors and ties no face to another.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-evidence`>>

!! The evidence that drew it (2026-10-18, lane `--w26-own-canon`)

* ''The shared document merges siblings' pointers.'' `oracleGenesisDocUrl()` gives every vessel one id, and each vessel writes its own `lares` and `lararium` urls, crossroads pin and realm pointers into it (`genesis-artifact.ts` `mintLaresIfAbsent`, `who-face.ts`, `realm-plane.ts`). Two vessels that each mint their own `lares` document settle on ONE `LARES_DOC_URI` once they sync; the control, never connected, keeps each its own (`packages/lararium-node/tests/shared-oracle-doc.probe.test.ts`, on lane `--w26-floor-1`).
* ''The split moves rather than closes.'' With the lares wiki's own canon held to its granted document, `herm-reshares-public-blob` step ③ failed three of three runs: the MOVE landed in A's document while `wiki which` and sync read the document the shared plane named. A's log in each run: `layer lar:///ha.ka.ba/bags/lararium moved …` then `layer lar:///ha.ka.ba/bags/lares held at …; the shared plane names …`. A's lares wiki then mounted the joinee's lararium corpus, the reverse of `W21`'s ruling.
* ''One signed floor object stands, and it names no Nexus.'' `OraclePointer` (domain `oracle-pointer`) signs a cid, heads and parents under the vessel seed (`oracle-substrate.ts`, `mountOracleReadFace`); readers pin the hearth's key.
* ''The system bags mount writable everywhere.'' `slotLayerFlags` marks every slot but `@oracle` writable, and every vessel registers `lares` and `lararium` as its own ground (`register-bags.ts` `OWN_GROUND`).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-slices`>>

!! The slices ~ each red-first, in the order what inherits shape from what

# ''The floor.'' A manifest CID names history-free snapshots of the `lares` and `lararium` rows beside the engine CIDs; `@oracle` mounts as its read-face, read-only, under every recipe; genesis bakes the first floor, its corpus CID in the seed, in one labelled `GENESIS RE-BAKE`. Red: a minted wiki reads a system carrier through `@oracle` and its write refuses.
# ''Pointers go home.'' Each vessel's own document pointers leave the shared document for its bootstrap; the shared `@oracle` document as a writable registry retires. `shared-oracle-doc.probe` (`packages/lararium-node/tests/shared-oracle-doc.probe.test.ts`) turns into this slice's red: two synced vessels each keep their own pointer. `W21` re-cuts here: a joinee mounts its hearth's floor by CID.
# ''The live system documents rest in the Kahu realm'', under persona zero's group until `K15`'s face context stands and the Kahu face's own group takes the seat (#/the-seam-owed-to-wave-c). A one-seat founding lands a counted 1-of-1 Kahu-cabal admission for persona zero's group; `kahuCabalCanonAccess` carries the production reads (`kahu-cabal-admission.ts`); `bags/` projects on the Kahu's vessel alone. A one-seat founding reads system canon through it over a founded island, a lone hearth's `working-loop` promotion lands, and a second vessel holds no live system document. Each document's id derives from the steward's seed and the Kahu canon counts only the steward's own sealed registrations (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu`>>).
# ''The release answer.'' One porch body per Nexus, sealed by the steward under its own signing domain, `floor-release` (operator-approved, 2026-10-18), the promotion approval over the manifest's exact bytes attached; the crossroads board carries it; `P8` folds in. `ORACLE_POINTER_DOMAIN` retires once releases stand.
# ''The accept act.'' A person's act moves a sealed hot row per (face, Nexus) to a newer release that verifies against that Nexus's charter at its epoch and links to the pinned release; no clock and no automatic accept. With no Nexus speaking, a face stands on its last accepted floor, or genesis.
# ''Reach into system canon.'' Every residency verb into `bags/lares` or `bags/lararium` refuses a hand outside the Kahu cabal, through the one predicate slice 3 seats; the skipped red in `unbuilt-laws.test.ts` ("a non-Kahu MOVE into a canon bag refuses") greens here.

''Beside the slices.'' `herm-reshares-public-blob` re-aims onto a public user bag: it tests a herm's re-share of a public blob, and a peer's write into system canon belongs to the Kahu alone. The re-aim travels to the mesh-goldens lane as a patch, beside the layer instrument that measured step ③.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-seam-owed-to-wave-c`>>

!! The seam owed to Wave C ~ one accessor names the Kahu realm's group

`kahuRealmGroup` (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/kahu-canon-reach.ts`) names the PersonaGroup that holds a Nexus's Kahu
realm. Persona zero's group holds it today. Wave C (`K15` step 6) swaps the Kahu face's own group in at that function
alone: the cabal's acts key on the group the accessor returns, and the realm's storage (the registrations on the
Nexus's realm doc) never keys on it, so the swap moves no data. Every consumer reads the group through the accessor,
never through a literal or a persona-zero lookup: the founding's admission (`landFoundingAdmission`), the daemon
island's source (`kahuReachSourceOf`) and the doors' one predicate (`requireKahuReach`).

''THE LINEAGE RIDES THE RECORD, NEVER THE CARD.'' The release record and the porch answer carry the charter's epoch lineage, so a herm and a browser verify a release holding the Nexus AID alone. A card holds 4096 characters and the lineage self-certifies against the AID, so the card stays small. The carrier changes nothing about trust: the AID that anchors the lineage comes from the card or the genesis CID the reader already pins, never from the same answer, and an answer self-consistent for another AID refuses (`foreign-nexus`).

''THE WIKI ISLAND TRUSTS THE GATE.'' A verb routed into a wiki island (`lares act --in-wiki`, `wiki-act`) carries its real
destination and source inside `args.args`, where the gate's cap derivation never looked. The keyholder island's
verify-then-delegate gate now asks the Kahu predicate for each system bag the envelope names (`routedInnerBags`,
`CapabilityVerifier.kahuReach`), before it routes. The wiki island holds no board and no sentinel, so its action reactors
run with `kahuReachClearedAtGate` and ask nothing again. The flag is a composition option set in code at the island's behavior, never a field of the envelope, so nothing a caller sends lifts it; every other composition leaves it unset and asks itself (`gateRoutedVerb`, `kahu-reach-gate-envelope.test`).
The live canon documents reach every reader through the realm's standing registration (`realmStandingDocUrl`): the
action reactors, `meme` and `where` by `systemCanonUrl`, and a recipe's library bag by `libraryDocUrl`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/what-retires`>>

!! What retires, slice by slice (the o-takiage census)

* RETIRED at slice 3: `lares` and `lararium` in every vessel's ground (`register-bags.ts` registers them only where the keel named canon), `mintLaresIfAbsent` / `mintLarariumIfAbsent` and `reconcileWellKnownTiddlers` (`genesis-artifact.ts`), and every catalog row for either bag. The founding mints and registers them in the Kahu realm (`kahu-realm.ts`).
* The shared document's per-vessel rows: the system pointers, `registerCrossroadsInOracle`, `realm-plane.ts`'s realm pointer and steward nym: slice 2.
* recipe-watch's oracle loop over the system slots (`recipe-watch.ts`): slice 2, which leaves it nothing to follow.
* `W21`'s lares-pointer patch at reconcile and the oracle crossing of `device-admit.ts` `admitCanonDocs`: slice 2.
* RETIRED at slice 4: `OraclePointer`, `ORACLE_POINTER_DOMAIN`, `snapshotPublicOracle` and the node's oracle floor serve. The floor answers by the release at the porch; the flow map keeps its own pointer under `FLOW_POINTER_DOMAIN` (`flow-pointer`), and `oracle-substrate`, `oracle-read-client` and `oracle-read-face` live on as `flow-substrate`, `flow-read-client` and `flow-read-face`. Left for an o-takiage pass: the route and gate names that still say "oracle" (`ORACLE_SOCKET_ROUTE`, `OracleFloorGate`, `OracleGateArgs`), which name the flow map's socket and not a pointer.
* Canon lines that named "the shared floor CID" now describe the release (`oracle-governance#/board-model`, `pronaos#/the-rung-ladder`, `genesis-doc#/the-oracle-pointer`, `disk-projection`, `dreamnet-architecture`); the docblocks that call the oracle "the vessel's own plane" or libraries "read-only from wiki" follow the slice whose code makes each line true.

''The interim cure stays out.'' The lane's recipe-watch hold (`--w26-own-canon`, commit `d1983aa75`) keeps a system wiki's own canon against a shared pointer that slice 2 removes; under //build the end state// it lands nowhere. Its probes stand: the shared-oracle probe as slice 2's red, the `W25` creator probe as that row's measurement.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/owed`>>

!! Owed beside the slices

* ''The `bags/oracle` → floor rename'' (the o-takiage left it by ruling). The genesis island document still carries the name `oracle` (`BAG_IDS.oracle`, `ORACLE_BAG`, `oracleUrl`, `lar:///ha.ka.ba/bags/oracle`, the `oracle` plane and recipe-uri segment, the `kind: "oracle"` row tag) because it is the floor's read-face layer under every wiki recipe. It renames together with the layer change that mounts the floor by the accepted pin (slice 5) and the genesis re-bake; `lararium-mesh/tests/o-takiage-names.test.ts` allowlists each such name with this row as its reason, so the list burns down with that lane.
* ''The carriers that still say oracle.'' `lararium/mesh/oracle-governance` (a governance design whose live lines now name the floor release and the flow pointer) and the carrier URIs cited widely keep their names; renaming a carrier moves every citation, so it rides its own lane. `api/pono/oracle` names the oracle sigil (divination), a different sense that stays.
* ''A browser Kahu hand.'' The browser composes no board reader and reads as a stranger, so it fails closed on every system bag. A browser vessel holding the Kahu face rides Wave C's face context (`K15` step 6), which carries the charter and the board to it.
* ''A sibling device of a seat-holding face'' (the row beside the browser Kahu hand). A joined device is a sibling of the Kahu's own face, so by law its reach follows the face's counted admission once the charter's board reads on it. Today it composes no board source, reads as a stranger and draws `kahu-reach` on a system bag: a missing source, not the law. `e2e/meme-two-vessel-bag` step eight keeps the law's expectation (B promotes into its own lares book) as a `test.fails` named for this row, so it turns red the day the row lands and the lander flips it; `device-admit.ts` states the law.
* ''The carriage's canon names'' (`device-admit.ts` `admitCanonDocs`, the browser's `hearth-canon`) read catalog rows nothing writes now; they retire with `W21`'s rest.
* ''The floor card's freshness.'' The browser verifies the lineage, the head release and the floor bytes from the Nexus AID alone (`browser-floor-read`). The AID reads off a verified floor pin claim (the card's claim, anchored apart from the card and folded to its place-KEL head) or an admit; the floor pin claim lane (`w14-floor-pin-lane`) orders the pin by KEL descent and carries it atomically with a counted carry.
* ''The accept act'' (slice 5) records no acceptance until a face's D row stands (Wave C's FCK fence); the browser's read records none.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-lane`>>

!! The lane ~ crew and crucible

* ''Branch'': `feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5--w26-floor-<n>`, one per slice or burst, cut from the span's head. Nothing lands on the span directly.
* ''Crew'': the work swarm (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/mixed-model-swarm`>> #/the-shapes): an Opus Episkopos, a Sol-6 and a Luna-6 in one provisioned worktree, walled per #/the-launch; spirits build and write red tests, and the lead runs every suite. Every slice moves facts between processes (a vessel and its islands, a vessel and its siblings), so the overwatch reads every burst.
* ''Shared ground'': both vessel composers. One lane at a time holds a composer hunk; the others hand theirs to the controller as a patch. Slice 1's re-bake and every plugin-bundle rebuild land on the controller's word.
* ''The crucible'': each touched package's whole suite under a scratch `HOME` and `LAR_ROOT`, `tools/typecheck-witness.sh`, one shard run with its `MERGE-VERIFY` line, a pull request into the span with no Copilot review unless the operator asks; the controller runs `tools/witness-all.sh` and merges.

<<~/ahu>>

